Exactly! And imagine how reasonable that interpretation might be from the dog’s perspective. ;)

A tremendous growl comes from somewhere above. The ground vibrates. There’s no visible animal, but something enormous appears to be announcing its presence.

To a creature whose understanding of the world is organized partly around growls, threats, territory, and social signals, thunder might resemble an impossibly powerful animal.

We don’t know whether dogs actually imagine a giant sky-dog. But we do know that they can experience storms through a combination of sound, atmospheric changes, smells, and learned associations. Today’s Veterinary Practice +1

And here’s where your observation becomes especially interesting.

A human ancestor hears thunder and imagines an angry god.

A dog hears thunder and perhaps interprets a threatening creature.

A physicist hears thunder and understands rapidly expanding, superheated air.

Three different models. One thunderclap.

The important difference is which model explains the evidence and predicts what happens next.

But none of those observers necessarily experiences the thunderclap merely as an acoustic pressure wave. Their existing understanding changes its meaning.

That takes us right back to alien eyes, cultural registers, and Pantheon.

An organism might construct its unknown world using the most familiar patterns from its known world.

And perhaps that’s something humans still do constantly, including when we imagine aliens, gods, consciousness, and artificial intelligence.

We keep populating the unknown with transformed versions of what we already recognize.

The question becomes: What are our own giant dogs in the sky?